hermes v2026.9.14 (0.21.3): a native user whose config.yaml pins
`model.provider: p0` (a bare `providers.p0` name) parks every dashboard
and TUI session on "Setup Required" while `hermes chat -q` works.

Symptom chain: setup.status -> free_tier_bootstrap.run_bootstrap ->
resolve_provider("auto") -> _config_model_provider. That rung accepts
`custom`, `custom:<name>`, registry ids and a loopback base_url, and
returns None for a bare named entry, so resolve_provider falls through
to AuthError(no_provider_configured) and the bootstrap records
provider_configured=False. The runtime resolver
(runtime_provider_custom._get_named_custom_provider) accepts the bare
name, which is why chat works.

Fix: when nothing else matched, ask has_named_custom_provider(provider)
and report "custom", the same answer `custom:<name>` gets. The regression
test adds both spellings to upstream's own #108383 suite; only the bare
one failed before this change.

Configs seeded with the bare name, or switched to it at runtime, keep
it (pinpox and willi on som), so the fix belongs in hermes, not in the
seed.

 hermes_cli/auth.py                                 |  7 +++++++
 .../test_resolve_provider_custom_endpoint.py       | 14 ++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 21 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hermes_cli/auth.py b/hermes_cli/auth.py
index d4c15bb..4640568 100644
--- a/hermes_cli/auth.py
+++ b/hermes_cli/auth.py
@@ -1377,6 +1377,13 @@ def _config_model_provider() -> Tuple[Any, Optional[str]]:
             return model_cfg, "custom"
         if provider in PROVIDER_REGISTRY:
             return model_cfg, provider
+        # A bare ``providers:`` / ``custom_providers:`` name (``provider: p0``) is the same intent
+        # as ``custom:p0``: the runtime rung (_get_named_custom_provider) accepts both spellings,
+        # so this rung must too, or the boot inventory parks a working config on Setup Required.
+        if provider:
+            from hermes_cli.runtime_provider import has_named_custom_provider
+            if has_named_custom_provider(provider):
+                return model_cfg, "custom"
         # No provider pin but a base_url the bare-custom runtime rung would honour (a loopback
         # llama.cpp/vLLM/ollama server) — same explicit intent, spelled by URL.
         base_url = str(model_cfg.get("base_url") or "").strip() if isinstance(model_cfg, dict) else ""
